call_details.response:
The position contains 10 pieces of each color with x to move. Columns 2 and (effectively) 5 are unusable: column 2 is already full, while a placement in column 5 immediately enables an o win on the existing three-in-a-row on row 4. Column 0 is likewise a dead zone; every 4-window that intersects it is already blocked by o or becomes blocked the moment the column is opened (gravity lets o occupy the critical square). Consequently the only candidate moves lie in columns 1, 3, 4 and 6.

A complete enumeration of remaining open 4-windows (windows containing no opponent stone) shows:
- (1,1) participates in only one window, and that window is unreachable in any reasonable number of moves;
- (5,6) participates in only the vertical window of column 6, a side-column threat that the opponent can spoil with a single interception;
- (1,4) participates in exactly one window—the long empty diagonal (0,3)–(1,4)–(2,5)–(3,6);
- (0,3) participates in that same diagonal plus the empty top-row window (0,3)–(0,4)–(0,5)–(0,6).

The extra window, even though it can be completed only late, still gives (0,3) the highest potential. Occupying it also prevents o from extending the lone stone already present on the top row. All shorter-term forcing sequences (the two lurking three-in-a-rows, possible vertical builds on the right, etc.) are either non-convertible because of gravity or are equally available after the move in column 3. Therefore the unique optimal placement is column 3.

Final Answer: 3

